In .NET 10 (and late .NET 9), `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` no longer works because Android deprecated `adjustResize`. MAUI now relies on **`KeyboardAutoManagerScroll`** which automatically scrolls focused inputs into view.

**Quick fix — make sure auto-scroll is enabled (it's on by default):**

```xml
<!-- No platform-specific code needed if your Entry/Editor is inside a ScrollView or layout that can shrink -->
```

If it's still hidden, the most common causes and fixes:

### 1. Ensure your layout can shrink

Wrap your page content in a `Grid` with a `*` row for content and `Auto` for the input bar:

```xml
<Grid RowDefinitions="*, Auto">
    <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" ... />
    <HorizontalStackLayout Grid.Row="1">
        <Entry Placeholder="Type a message..." HorizontalOptions="FillAndExpand" />
        <Button Text="Send" />
    </HorizontalStackLayout>
</Grid>
```

### 2. If auto-scroll isn't working, opt back in explicitly

In `MauiProgram.cs`:

```csharp
builder
    .UseMauiApp<App>()
    .ConfigureMauiHandlers(handlers =>
    {
#if ANDROID
        // Ensure the keyboard auto-manager is active (default is true)
        Microsoft.Maui.Platform.KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Disconnect = false;
#endif
    });
```

### 3. If you need the old resize behavior on Android

In `Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs`:

```csharp
using Android.Views;
using AndroidX.Core.View;

protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
{
    base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

    // Use WindowInsetsCompat (the modern replacement for adjustResize)
    WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(Window!, false);

    ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(Window!.DecorView, new InsetsListener());
}

private class InsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        v.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, imeInsets.Bottom);
        return insets;
    }
}
```

This listens for IME (keyboard) insets and adds bottom padding to push your content up — the modern equivalent of `adjustResize`.

**TL;DR:** The default `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll` should handle it. If not, use the `WindowInsetsCompat` approach in option 3 for full control.
